<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Route 4 &middot; you build it</div>
		<h1>Encrypted</h1>
		<p class="lede">Full-disk encryption with an on-screen keyboard at boot. It
		works on this phone, and there is no image to download.</p>
		<p>Like every route here, it needs the phone unlocked at Xiaomi first
		&mdash; a Mi account and seven days of waiting.
		<a href="unlock.html">The steps</a>.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>Why there is no download</h2></div>
		<p>A LUKS master key is created when the disk is formatted, which for an image
		means on the machine that builds it. Changing the passphrase later replaces the
		slot protecting that key, and leaves the key itself untouched. A shared
		encrypted image would give
		whoever built it the master key of every phone flashed from it.</p>
		<p>Yours has to be born on your machine. One command.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>02</span><h2>What you need</h2></div>
		<p><b>A Linux PC.</b> pmbootstrap builds inside chroots on loop devices,
		which only Linux has, and its own documentation rules out the Windows
		subsystem for Linux by name. On a Mac or a Windows machine, run a Linux
		virtual machine and work inside it. Flashing a ready-made image is a
		different matter and works from all three.</p>
		<p>About <b>15 GB free</b> and <b>an hour</b>, most of it the kernel. And these,
		which the script checks before it starts:</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Debian, Ubuntu</th><td class="mono">sudo apt install python3 git rsync openssl kpartx util-linux procps tar</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Fedora</th><td class="mono">sudo dnf install python3 git rsync openssl kpartx util-linux procps-ng tar</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Arch</th><td class="mono">sudo pacman -S python git rsync openssl multipath-tools util-linux procps-ng tar</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>Nothing else has to be set up first: <code>setup-workdir</code> fetches
		pmbootstrap and pmaports itself, and makes you a signing key of your own.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>03</span><h2>Build it</h2></div>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON YOUR LINUX PC</span></div>
			<pre>git clone https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports
cd utsugi-pmaports
scripts/setup-workdir
scripts/build-your-own-image --encrypted</pre>
		</div>
		<p>pmbootstrap asks <b>you</b> for the passphrase during the install. Then
		flash it like any other image, to <code>userdata</code>.</p>
		<p>Verified end to end here: the initramfs opens a LUKS that lives inside a
		loop file on <code>userdata</code>, and <code>unl0kr</code> draws its keyboard
		on this panel.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>04</span><h2>Why it asks for your password</h2></div>
		<p>A script you cloned five minutes ago asking for <code>sudo</code> is a
		fair reason to stop. Here is every use of it, and you can check each one
		with <code>grep -n sudo scripts/*</code> and in
		<a href="https://gitlab.postmarketos.org/postmarketOS/pmbootstrap">pmbootstrap</a>'s
		own source.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Who</th><th>What</th><th>Why it cannot be done as you</th></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">setup-workdir</td><td>one <code>chown</code> of the
			signing key to uid 12345</td><td>Giving a file to another user is the one
			thing only root can do. Builds run as <code>pmos</code> (uid 12345) inside
			the chroot; a key only you can read fails at the last step, after
			everything has compiled.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">pmbootstrap</td><td><code>chroot</code>,
			<code>mount</code>, <code>mknod</code></td><td>The build environment is a
			directory with a real <code>/proc</code>, <code>/sys</code> and device
			nodes bind-mounted into it. Entering it, and creating those, are
			privileged operations on every Linux.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">pmbootstrap</td><td><code>losetup</code>,
			<code>parted</code>, <code>partprobe</code></td><td>The image is a file
			treated as a disk: attaching it as a loop device and writing a partition
			table to it go through the kernel's block layer.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">pmbootstrap</td><td><code>cryptsetup luksFormat</code>,
			<code>mkfs</code>, <code>dd</code></td><td>Creating the encrypted container
			and the filesystems inside it, and writing the boot image. This is the step
			that generates <b>your</b> master key, on <b>your</b> machine.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>What none of it does: install anything on your system, write outside
		<code>~/.local/var/pmbootstrap-utsugi</code>, or touch your
		<code>/etc</code>. The work directory is ordinary files you can delete with
		<code>rm -rf</code> when you are done, and the loop devices go away with it.</p>
		<p>If you would rather not grant any of this, the ready-made image needs
		none of it &mdash; only <code>fastboot</code>. What it cannot give you is an
		encrypted disk, for the reason in step 01.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>05</span><h2>What it costs</h2></div>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>A stuck kernel workqueue takes the whole filesystem with it.</b>
			On 2026-09-10 an ADSP crash whose recovery deadlocked left this phone frozen
			for seven hours with the root inside dm-crypt. The trigger is fixed, and a
			watchdog now reboots and saves the kernel log to a partition, but the cause
			is still open.
		</div></div>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>Encrypting an install you already have is not offered.</b>
			It needs the filesystem shrunk, cannot run on the mounted root, and ends with
			initramfs, <code>fstab</code> and <code>crypttab</code> rewritten by hand.
			Back up, reinstall encrypted, restore.
		</div></div>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="install-image.html"><b>Simpler</b><span>The ready image</span></a>
			<a href="devices.html"><b>Read</b><span>What does not work</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
